{"format":"tvf-scene","v":1,"name":"example · addresses given, payloads met","loop":32,"ease":"inout","hold":0,"gravity":1,"wind":0,"trail":null,"camera":[{"t":0,"box":[-0.153,-0.473,0.117,-0.197]},{"t":0.46,"box":[-0.153,-0.473,0.117,-0.197]},{"t":0.5,"box":[-0.1672,-0.4872,0.2172,-0.1828]},{"t":0.64,"box":[-0.1672,-0.4872,0.2172,-0.1828]},{"t":0.71,"box":[-0.1947994,-0.5147994,0.2447994,0.6947894]},{"t":0.8,"box":[-0.1947994,-0.5147994,0.2447994,0.6947894]},{"t":0.85,"box":[-0.1672,-0.4872,0.2172,-0.1828]}],"stops":[0.14,0.3,0.46,0.64,0.8],"beats":[{"title":"A hand at home","text":"Stand at *home* and hold up a hand, fingers spread from flat to upright. Turn evenly from the first finger to the last and each one points a fixed step further round: here twelve of them, 7.5° apart.\n\nThe fingers are where something *can* be put. They were laid by the turn alone, before anything was seen, and the essay calls them **addresses**."},{"title":"A window paints them","text":"Hold the hand up to a stained-glass window. Each finger lines up with one pane and is lit by the colour that pane lets through.\n\nThat colour is the **payload**: whatever arrives along a finger's line. It tells you about the window. It says nothing about how far off the glass is, or how large."},{"title":"Another window, the same fingers","text":"Move the window twice as far away and make it twice as large. Every pane lines up with the same finger as before, and **every finger is lit exactly as it was**: no colour carries a distance.\n\nNow change the window. Every colour changes. Not one finger moves. The addresses belong to the hand; only the payload belongs to the window."},{"title":"Laid on a line: the Cauchy","text":"Carry each finger on until it meets a straight line one length *H* away. Finger *k* lands at *H*·tan θ, its reading v/h = tan θ.\n\nThe turn was even, but the landings are not: they crowd near the foot of the line and spread out as they climb. The law of where they land is the Cauchy, 2 ⁄ π(1 + s²), drawn beside the line. Half the fingers turn less than 45°, so **half land below *H***: the corner, v/h = 1, is the median. [The essay, §5](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/03-addresses-given-payloads-met.md)"},{"title":"A median, and no mean","text":"Pull back. The last finger, 3.75° short of upright, lands fifteen lengths up; with twice the fingers the last lands thirty up, and the line runs on toward the *horizon*, which no finger reaches.\n\nThe median of the readings stays at 1 however many fingers there are. Their mean does not settle: 2.55 for twelve fingers, 2.99 for twenty-four, 3.43 for forty-eight, about 0.44 more each time the fingers double. **The missing mean belongs to the turn**, not to anything seen along it."},{"title":"Change what arrives, and no address moves","text":"Now let amounts arrive at the landings, drawn as bars: one window's light, then another window's, then the first again. Every bar changes. **Not one landing moves**, and the corner stays where the turn put it.\n\nThat is the essay's theorem in plain words: the Cauchy is the law of the addresses, fixed by where you stand and how you turn, and nothing the world sends can change it. It is why practice takes the median of such readings and not the mean. [Addresses given, payloads met](https://github.com/reportbase/wander/blob/main/papers/essays/03-addresses-given-payloads-met.md)"}],"path":null,"items":[{"fill":{"color":"#f4f1ea","opacity":1},"stroke":null,"width":0,"open":false,"exact":{"x":[-0.9,0.9,0.9,-0.9],"y":[0.9,0.9,-0.9,-0.9],"r":null,"smooth":null,"ctrl":null,"holes":[]}},{"morph":0},{"morph":1},{"morph":3},{"morph":4},{"morph":5},{"morph":6},{"morph":7},{"morph":8},{"morph":9},{"morph":10},{"morph":11},{"morph":12},{"morph":13},{"morph":14},{"morph":15},{"morph":16},{"morph":17},{"morph":18},{"morph":19},{"morph":20},{"morph":21},{"morph":22},{"morph":23},{"morph":24},{"morph":25},{"morph":26},{"morph":27},{"morph":28},{"morph":29},